	}, {
		name = "ON THE STATUS OF AUTOPLAY";
		text = "WE DO NOT, AND WILL NOT FACILITATE OR GRANT SPECIAL PRIVILEGES TO AUTOPLAYERS \n \nIt is not in our interest to support, facilitate, or accord special status to autoplayers. Nothing we have implemented into the game is indicative of that notion. The hide auto tag gamepass we implemented last Christmas does not accord special status to auto players. All it does is remove one BillboardGui above your player’s head that says you’re autoplaying. And just for this simple function alone, we’ve made the price of this gamepass the most expensive in our lineup; more expensive than competing piano games. \n \nThe only reason why the gamepass was created is to satisfy a growing demand from the playerbase; one that grew too large to ignore. And the analytics we’ve gotten since its inception has only further substantiated this: the hide auto tag gamepass is presently the best selling gamepass in our lineup by a wide margin. Many players are buying the pass in spite of its comparatively higher cost; which is indicative of its huge demand from the playerbase. We will always put the interests of the playerbase first. \nNO INCENTIVE TO ENCOURAGE AUTOPLAY \n \nThere’s nothing more irritable than walking into Lobby and seeing someone autoplaying a Rush E midi at 200 BPM with Z-Doc font at 200% volume. Believe it or not, we despise it too. It’s not in our interest to encourage this; and we have nothing to gain from enabling behaviour like this. \n \nThere is a common notion that the gamepass was created for maximising our profit; and this is correct. But unlike similar competitors, we do not put auto-playing front and centre as our selling proposition. It’s why [FREE AUTO] is no longer in our name. It’s also why we offer a range of other gamepasses (all of which boast a wider variety of more complex features such as VIP and 2x points) — all of which are more affordable than the hide autotag gamepass. \n \nWe have not coerced or enabled autoplayers by limiting user choices; on the contrary, we’ve provided a variety of avenues by which users may upgrade their Visual Pianos experience; plenty of which do not involve paying-to-win. As an addendum, paying-to-win would be completely unstrategic to our profitability; we thrive on the sustainability of a freemium model, and facilitating the interests of our players is core to that strategy. Any notion that we’re forcing players to pay-to-win would be without basis; if anything, we are making autoplayers pay-to-win; not punishing regular players, who practice to get better.";
	}, {
		name = "ON THE STATUS OF EXPLOITERS";
		text = "WE DO NOT ENCOURAGE, ENABLE OR CONDONE EXPLOITING IN ANY WAY \n \nExploiting is against the Roblox Terms of Use and Visual Pianos Studios, like any other development team on the platform, is liable to those regulations. Exploiting, by definition, is script injection into one’s client. Any other form of third party software that does not inject into, or modify the client, is not a form of exploiting. \n \nRoblox does not prohibit the usage of such software, such as midi input applications. The usage of such applications does not involve script injection into the Roblox client, and as such, do not qualify as exploiting. To this end, our active team of volunteer staff have always been instructed to take action against exploiters, should definitive proof exist of script injection having taken place. This has been the case for years, and it will continue to be so. \n \nTHE IMPRACTICALITY OF IDENTIFYING AUTOPLAYING EXPLOITERS \n \nThe best and frontmost form of exploit detection is the Roblox client itself. If an exploiter using an executor is able to bypass that and inject into their client, there’s realistically very little that any game can do to definitively identify an exploiter. Some common symptoms of exploiting include significant ping spikes potentially arising from injection. Most anti-exploits use this metric to identify potential exploiters, such as Adonis. However, ping spikes can be caused by a variety of other factors as well, such as an unstable Internet connection; so this does not definitively prove that exploiting has occurred. \n \nAnother common notion is that exploiters can be identified by how they’re playing; such as consistent tempo, equidistant notes, notes of the same length, or just playing too many notes in general. However, all of this is achievable without script injection, by means of midi input software like MIRP, MidiToQwerty and MIDI++. Hence, this is not definitive proof of exploiting. \n \nIn our efforts to identify exploiting autoplayers, we’ve also experimented with AFK detection, cursor exiting the Roblox window while notes are being played, among other things. However, none of this has been able to definitively identify exploiting auto-players, as these are all achievable with the use of midi input software; not necessarily script injection. \n \nWe will not condone any form of exploiting in our game, and take a firm stance against any attempts to do so. However, due to the impracticality of definitively identifying someone who’s exploiting — as opposed to using midi software — we are unable to take any action on autoplayers, unless definitive proof of exploiting is forthcoming (i.e. they start flying, speed hacking or using other common IY commands). \n \nIt is therefore unfair to assume that a player is exploiting solely based on common symptoms associated with exploiting, if none of them definitively prove that script injection has occurred. We encourage all players to share any suggestions that we may have overlooked on possible measures that can be taken to definitively identify exploiting autoplayers. \n \nDISRUPTIVE AUTOPLAYERS AND BLACK MIDI PLAYERS \n \nWe are aware that there exists a significant demographic of black MIDI enthusiasts within our community, and autoplaying is often utilised to facilitate the playing of black MIDIs on our game. Our stance is that so long as these MIDIs aren’t disruptive to the experience of other players (causing server lag, etc), they aren’t against the rules. In addition, our PianoModule places users on timeout should it detect that too many notes are being played. This means that other users will not be able to hear what’s being played for a period of time. Our staff have also been instructed to take action against disruptive players. We strongly encourage players who intend to play black MIDIs to do this in a private server instead, where you won’t be timed out or otherwise disruptive to other players.";
	}, {
		name = "RESOLUTION";
		text = "RESOLUTION \n \nAutoplaying is, at its core, not disruptive to the game’s experience. It is a tool by which many use to experiment with sheets, to transcribe new sheets, to preview our selection of sound fonts, among other things. There is nothing inherently wrong with this — nor is it abused as often as people think. We have provided plenty of avenues by which users can autoplay without the need for script injection, such as making autoplaying free, and supporting volunteer developers within our community by promoting successful midi software like MidiToQwerty and MIDI++. We think that it’s wholly impractical for users to risk account deletion by exploiting solely to autoplay; and it’s therefore unlikely that exploiters would inject their client just to autoplay. \n \nTherefore, allow us to set the record straight: autoplaying is not against the rules; exploiting and disruption is. We will not take any action against users who are autoplaying, unless they are actively disrupting the experience of other players, by exploiting, lagging the server, or otherwise. We will continue to explore every possible definitive measure to further identify, deter and penalise exploiters, including exploiting autoplayers. Our staff will continue to uphold the integrity of the game and maintain a fair and positive experience for all our players, by penalising exploiters where definitive evidence of script injection can be forthcoming.";
	}};
})]]></ProtectedString><BinaryString name="AttributesSerialize"></BinaryString><bool name="DefinesCapabilities">false</bool><string name="Name"><![CDATA[BookHandler]]></string><BinaryString name="Tags"></BinaryString></Properties></Item><Item class="ModuleScript" referent="21794"><Properties><ProtectedString name="Source"><![CDATA[-- Saved by UniversalSynSaveInstance (Join to Copy Games) https://discord.gg/wx4ThpAsmw

